(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2026-002"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2026-002","title":"Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2026","num":2,"category":"advance","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/004_Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the value of the following expression?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/436737c36100a8d5650e.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/775815f36c5b93c9de4f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2c50c1196422ee583228.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bcf1e09681b6427fc176.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59dd3055cfcf5696b1fe.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74a6e238e594a509ba24.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A student took five papers in an examination, where the full marks were the same for each paper. His marks in these papers were in the proportion of 6 : 7 : 8 : 9 : 10. In all papers together, the candidate obtained 60% of the total marks. Then the number of papers in which he got more than 50% marks is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d528eeb164a6e990d6a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><em>R</em>, <em>S</em> and <em>H</em> enter into a partnership and their capitals are in the proportion of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/3410e67a2b48cb45032d.png\" /> <em>R</em> withdraws half his capital at the end of 4 months. Out of total annual profit of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/ae6b9218502367c98161.png\" /> <em>R</em>’s share is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d38e0c4d792cbdaaa8f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4a/4a9adc4b7c36f4e079f2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/668dbdf8a91ec7da4bd2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/4259526ccd3a7fe25ee4.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea375a202e3afcb07691.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For this question, you have to base your conclusion on the three premises (viz: 1, 2 and 3). Assuming the facts in the statements to be true, which of the following conclusions logically follows from the statements?<br />\n<strong>Statements:</strong><br />\n1. Some cakes are bikes<br />\n2. Some bikes are hills<br />\n3. All laptops are bikes<br />\n<strong>Conclusions:</strong><br />\nI. All laptops are hills<br />\nII. Some laptops are cakes<br />\nIII. Some cakes are hills<br />\nIV. Some laptops are not cakes</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only I follows"},{"key":"B","html":"Only either II or IV follows"},{"key":"C","html":"Only I or II follows"},{"key":"D","html":"Only I and IV follows"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be9bff9429bd5d0aed43.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A messenger was returning to his base station which was in front of him to the north. When this base station was 100 m away from him, he turned to the left and moved 50 m to deliver the last message to the Peshwa’s troops. He then moved in the same direction for 40 m, turned to his<br />\nright and moved 100 m. How many meters away he is now from his base station?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"150"},{"key":"C","html":"90"},{"key":"D","html":"100"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e7b20176c1c05550a5e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A person starts typing the numbers from 1 to 1999. He press the keys total ‘n’ number of times.<br />\nThe value of <em>n</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6889"},{"key":"B","html":"1000"},{"key":"C","html":"2888"},{"key":"D","html":"none of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for one digit number = 9<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for two digit numbers = 90 &times; 2 = 180<br />\nThe numbers of times he pressed the keys for three digit numbers = 900 &times; 3 = 2700<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for the numbers 1000 to 1999 is 1000 &times; 4 = 4000<br />\nThus, total number of times the keys are pressed = 6889</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Choose the most appropriate word from the options given below to complete the following sentence.<br />\nBased on his recent poor decisions, it was obvious that Seth lacked even a _____ of good sense.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"debasement"},{"key":"B","html":"diversion"},{"key":"C","html":"disapprobation"},{"key":"D","html":"modicum"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nModicum is a small or token amount. It effectively conveys that Seth lacked even a small amount of good sense.</p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Below is a sentence of which some parts have been jumbled up. Rearrange these parts which are labelled <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em> and <em>S</em> to produce the correct sentence. Choose the proper sequence. It would<br />\nP : that they were quite in the dark<br />\nQ : about the policy of management<br />\nR : appear from their statement<br />\nS : in dealing with the strike<br />\nThe proper sequence should be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"S R P Q"},{"key":"B","html":"R P Q S"},{"key":"C","html":"S Q R P"},{"key":"D","html":"R S Q P"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&ldquo;It would appear ...&rdquo; can be the only coherent beginning of the sentence. Thus the first part<br />\nwould be <em>R</em>. Since R says &ldquo;their statement&rdquo;, sentence P would follow it because it also says, &ldquo;that they ...&rdquo;<br />\nThus, option (b) is the correct answer.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If you are a brisk walker, there is no need to go to a gym. Your neighbourhood can become your gym. You don’t need fancy equipment to get a good exercise either. All you need is a welldesigned pair of shoes.<br />\nThe paragraph best supports the statement that</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"poorly designed shoes can cause major foot injuries."},{"key":"B","html":"walking outdoors provides a better workout than walking indoors."},{"key":"C","html":"a gym membership is a poor investment."},{"key":"D","html":"brisk walking is a convenient and valuable form of exercise"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, C, B, A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nBy stating that brisk walking does not require commute to a gym, the author stresses the<br />\nconvenience of this form of exercise. The paragraph also states that brisk walking may result in a good workout.<br />\nOption (c) is not appropriate. A gym membership can be a good investment for some.<br />\nOption (b) is not given in the passage.<br />\nOption (a) seems logical but the paragraph does not mention it.<br />\nOption (d) is the most appropriate option.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>How many triangles are there in the following figure?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aaf7f592fbedede41d37.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6"},{"key":"B","html":"10"},{"key":"C","html":"11"},{"key":"D","html":"12"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74beba64b6ff2d8dac2e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em> be a continuous random variable where</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02d5e8e73b95f8a73af4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><em>E</em>[<em>X</em>] = ________. [Upto two decimal places]</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2.91,2.92],"answerText":"2.916","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2.916 [2.91 - 2.92]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/26d7bec9502feb6eac8c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Assume a system with cache line size of 64 bytes and only a 2 byte word can be transferred from cache to main memory or vice-versa at a time and this one word transfer takes 20 ns. Assume none of the cache block is empty. A process writes 20 words one by one, 10 words belong to cache block <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and 10 other words belong to cache block <em>C</em><sub>2</sub>. After this <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> are swapped out. If system uses write-through policy, then n is number of times <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> copied into main memory. If system uses write-back policy, then m is number of times <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> copied in main memory. The value of <em>n</em> – <em>m</em> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[18,18],"answerText":"18","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>18</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e34dffb60d90bc13082.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system with 2 way set-associative cache of 16 cache blocks and uses LRU replacement algorithm. Assume cache is initially empty. A process accesses following memory blocks in order:<br />\n0, 5, 31, 8, 45, 16, 5, 8, 0, 29, 5, 39, 15, 45, 8, 31<br />\nWhich of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 conflict misses experienced by cache."},{"key":"B","html":"5 hits experienced by cache."},{"key":"C","html":"Block 8 never removed from cache block due to any miss."},{"key":"D","html":"Block 15 and 45 exist in cache after all requests and in same set."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2ce405bf36121263baac.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33c8ea67b0ebcd381eee.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the 4 processes <em>P</em><sub>0</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>3</sub> with following details:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa2a9cfd60b7a3e7f8b5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Where 0 is highest priority. CPU schedular uses preemptive priority scheduling. The average turnaround time for above processes is ________ ms.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8.25,8.25],"answerText":"8.25","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8.25</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c138df96052979937c38.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The TCP end point typically send an RST segment?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"when closing connection gracefully."},{"key":"B","html":"when the sender wants to extend the connection time out."},{"key":"C","html":"when the congestion window becomes full."},{"key":"D","html":"when receiving a segment for a connection that does not exist."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nTCP sends RST segment, when a packet arrives for non-existent or invalid connection.</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> are two events such that <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/829b1a5a0eee1862a780.png\" /></p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.625,0.625],"answerText":"0.625","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.625</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f409fd52a8194bd27e7a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a system with 16 MB byte addressable physical memory 16 KB cache memory and cache block size is 32 bytes. Tag field length is 13 bits. The mapping of cache is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Direct mapped"},{"key":"B","html":"4 way set associative"},{"key":"C","html":"8 way set associative"},{"key":"D","html":"2 way set associative"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/8237670c70d4fc98981d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Half duplex transmission means</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It is strict condition that one side data is transmitted and other side ack is transmitted only."},{"key":"B","html":"Piggybacking can be applied if other side data is transmitted."},{"key":"C","html":"Both sides data is transmitted at same time."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIt is not a strict condition that only ack is transmitted on other side. If other system is having data<br />\nto transmit, it can add data to the ACK, so that bandwidth can be effectively utilized.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following two schedules of three transactions <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>T</em><sub>3</sub>:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d7151aee3c8186094fb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the schedules are conflicts serializable?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> only"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> both"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4fa08e37ee697bab732b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following are undecidable?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A string S is an element of a recursive language L."},{"key":"B","html":"A Turing machine halts on 75<sup>th</sup> step."},{"key":"C","html":"A Turing machine enters a given state within 115 steps."},{"key":"D","html":"A string S is accepted by a Turing machine M."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n(a) Membership problem of recursive language is decidable.<br />\n(b) This can be decided because of finite steps (75).<br />\n(c) This can be decided because of finite steps (115).<br />\n(d) Membership problem of TM cannot be decidable. A string may half or not on TM. And halting<br />\nproblem is undecidable.</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a real-time video streaming application using UDP, packets are occasionally lost due to network congestion. The developer decides to implement a custom reliability mechanism at the application layer to handle packet loss.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It preserves UDP’s low latency while improving reliability, but increases complexity at the application layer."},{"key":"B","html":"It reduces latency and complexity, since packet loss is handled by the network layer."},{"key":"C","html":"It makes UDP connection-oriented and identical to TCP, eliminating all packet loss."},{"key":"D","html":"It simplifies design but increases transmission delay, as UDP."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following C function:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9e8717235c4c0cd67a99.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The time complexity of above function is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"θ(log (<em>m</em>.<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"B","html":"θ(log <em>m</em> + log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"C","html":"θ((log <em>m</em>) ⋅ (log <em>n</em>))"},{"key":"D","html":"θ(<em>m</em> log <em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee13fe162e3384b97de4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The minimum number of temporary variables required to create a three-address code in static single assignment form for the expression <em>a</em> / <em>b</em> * <em>3</em> – <em>7</em> * <em>c</em> + <em>d</em> / 2 – e is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[7,7],"answerText":"7","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>7</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac4a0463fe291d071276.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following SMTP commands is used to initiate the transfer of the email content?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"DATA"},{"key":"B","html":"MAIL"},{"key":"C","html":"RCPT"},{"key":"D","html":"SEND"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nSMTP commands:<br />\n<strong>&bull; MAIL FROM:</strong> Begins a new mail transaction and identifies the sender.<br />\n<strong>&bull; RCPT TO:</strong> Identifies the recipient of the message.<br />\n<strong>&bull; DATA</strong> Indicates that the email content will now be sent.<br />\n<strong>&bull; SEND</strong> A rarely used command to send directly to a terminal-based user.</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a relation schema <em>R</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>, <em>D</em>) with functional dependencies <em>AB</em> → <em>C</em>, <em>BD</em> → <em>AC</em>, <em>A</em> → <em>D</em>.<br />\n<em>R</em> into <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>A</em>, <em>D</em>), <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>).<br />\nWhich of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> decomposition is lossless join."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> decomposition is dependency preserving."},{"key":"C","html":"<em>R</em> is in 3NF but not in BCNF."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>R</em> has two candidate keys."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef355f98b45443418192.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Suppose a database has following relation instances:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/770157d8bdd56318bc7d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Where underlined attributes are primary key.<br />\nConsider the following SQL query:<br />\nSELECT coach, Count (Sid)<br />\nFROM student S, Game G<br />\nWHERE S.Gid = G.Gid<br />\nGROUP BY coach;<br />\nThe number of tuples return by above SQL query is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3<br />\nQuery finds the coaches which associated with atleast one student and number of students associated with these coaches.<br />\nOnly coach &lsquo;PS&rsquo; is not associated with any student and total coach.<br />\nSo, 4 &ndash; 1 = 3</p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following correctly describe the below ER diagram?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/aec04349ef7172893be4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"An entity in <em>E</em><sub>1</sub> is associated with zero or more number of entities of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>. Every entity of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub> is associated with exactly one entity of <em>E</em><sub>1</sub>."},{"key":"B","html":"An entity in <em>E</em><sub>1</sub> is associated with zero or more number of entities of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>. An entity of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub> is associated with atmost one entity of <em>E</em><sub>1</sub>."},{"key":"C","html":"An entity in <em>E</em><sub>1</sub> is associated with atmost one entities of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>. An entity of <em>E</em><sub>2</sub> is associated with zero or more number of entities of <em>E</em><sub>1</sub>."},{"key":"D","html":"Every entity in <em>E</em><sub>1</sub> is associated with exactly one entity in <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>. An entity in <em>E</em><sub>2</sub> is associated with atmost one entity in <em>E</em><sub>1</sub>."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n<em>E</em><sub>1</sub> to <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>&nbsp;is one-to-many and <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>&nbsp;with total participation. Total participation says every entity in <em>E</em><sub>2</sub><br />\nparticipate and one-to-many says atmost one. So exactly one association.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> is context-free language and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is regular language. Which of the following languages are definitely a context free language:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3f44de6648be1f30455d.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>L<sub>x</sub></em>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>L<sub>z</sub></em>"},{"key":"C","html":"Only <em>L<sub>x</sub></em> and <em>L<sub>y</sub></em>"},{"key":"D","html":"Only <em>L<sub>y</sub></em> and <em>L<sub>z</sub></em>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33d5bf810989e0e3ffd1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of following features is commonly provided by a Next Generation Firewall but not by traditional firewall?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Basic NAT and port forwarding."},{"key":"B","html":"Packet filtering based only on source and destination IP."},{"key":"C","html":"Deep packet inspection and application level control."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nNext-generation firewall works from Layer 3 to Layer 7.</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider following two languages over Σ = {a, b}:</p>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cbcad3941c533b1b5d7d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are incorrect?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f413651ae0b43762af3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35145b746d1410e09c03.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac43750ae07863b21518.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/ccdcf36baf60e9d8ea82.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c,d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d7540994be4b67252314.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following DFAs M and <em>N</em> over Σ = {a, b}:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4ce7b6fc12bc7ceeb2d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following regular expression is equivalent to <em>L</em>(<em>M</em>) ∩ <em>L</em>(<em>N</em>)?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>ab</em> (<em>b</em> + <em>ab</em>)* <em>a</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>a</em> (<em>b</em> + <em>ba</em>)* <em>a</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>a</em> (<em>b</em> + <em>ab</em>)* <em>a</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/1336c54a5b507a2cf122.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/305bbdcd75f2458873c9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":32,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let regular expressions <em>r</em><sub>1</sub> = (aaa)* and <em>r</em><sub>2</sub> = (aaaa)* over Σ = {a}. Consider a DFA M such that<br />\n<em>L</em>(<em>M</em>) = <em>L</em>(<em>r</em><sub>1</sub>) ∩ <em>L</em>(<em>r</em><sub>2</sub>). The minimum number of states possible in M are ________.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d015b00be95b5766b953.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar over the alphabet Σ = {a, b}, where S is start state:</p>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e11d56350a69dfbeb594.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following language accepted by above grammar?</div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/834125e0259c73ed02d2.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/bec3c7c123bd4516014e.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6df4dc6d1b0c9ac6182.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/626261d5b1e0e0d77667.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4efc6aef0aa03c28632.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a disk with 100 tracks numbered from 0 to 99. Disk uses shortest-seek-time-first algorithm to schedule the requests. Assume R/W head is placed at track number 50. Disk has following track requests (in track numbers):<br />\n10, 20, 43, 65, 69, 79<br />\nR/W head takes 2 ms to move from one track to its adjacent track. Total time taken by disk to service the requests given above is (Assume time to data transfer for a request is negligible</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"224 ms"},{"key":"B","html":"112 ms"},{"key":"C","html":"109 ms"},{"key":"D","html":"218 ms"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/341012f7ec1ca23e5135.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system has 5 processes and each process requires maximum 8 resources. Find the minimum number of resources in system such that system free from deadlock?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[36,36],"answerText":"36","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>36</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/93bad5244d39f561a588.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system allocate fixed four frames to a process P. Process P references following pages in given sequence:<br />\n1, 5, 3, 8, 3, 6, 5, 1, 7, 8, 3, 5, 1, 3, 5<br />\nLet n and m are number of page faults occurs for First-In-First-Out (FIFO) and Least-Recently- Used (LRU) page replacement policies respectively used by system on execution of P as shown above. The value of n + m is ________. (Initially frames are empty)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[20,20],"answerText":"20","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>20</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b70b13bc02228d4cfaa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following Karnaugh map of function <em>f</em> (<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>, <em>d</em>):</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/97/973614faa136bd50ee3f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d2930f256c986a84f81.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e2b35f9a7b164cfc44c.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1599b15f39eb46817394.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/abf90bd8858628a60911.png\" />"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/2661ac5e6d9401d50936.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the circuit given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b2fbd315efdb9f7b848.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Where <em>F</em> = <em>bc</em>′, then value of <em>x</em> is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"a"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>c</em>′"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64109c7d7f7df3593bea.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Assume a 32 bit register <em>R</em> contains a floating-point number in IEEE-754 single precision notation. R contains 7EE80000 in hexadecimal notation. Let <em>D</em><sub>1</sub> is decimal value of R. And <em>D</em><sub>2</sub> is minimum possible positive decimal number that can be represented in IEEE-754 single precision format. The value of <em>D</em><sub>1</sub> × <em>D</em><sub>2</sub> in decimal is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.8125,1.8125],"answerText":"1.8125","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.8125</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85d998d45766d8e0a1ae.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f6/f696eebc736e2061037f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following synchronous circuit:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b92d8630894443d959d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>State of above counter represented as <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub><em>Q</em><sub>1</sub><em>Q</em><sub>0</sub>. Following is state diagram for above circuit.\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/52/528ab88e4f0eeeec3b8e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are correct?</div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35295313f8490d3ee3ab.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f2bb85377056964d90f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f3/f3a24a2f433627596976.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/944238c65944e0027254.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e747f2398089478feb3f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"LALR(1) parser and SLR(1) parser have same number of conflict for the same grammar."},{"key":"B","html":"If LR(1) parser had no S-R conflicts, then the corresponding derived LALR(1) parser will also have none."},{"key":"C","html":"LALR(1) parser have exactly the same number of states as SLR(1) parser for the same grammar."},{"key":"D","html":"LALR(1) parser may perform a few more reductions but not shifts than LR(1) parser before detecting an error in a string."}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)<br />\n&bull; LALR(1) may have few conflicts, as compare to SLR(1) parser. That is why it is more powerful than SLR(1).<br />\n&bull; LALR(1) depends on core of item (left part of item), not on lookaheads. So, same number of states as LR(1).<br />\n&bull; On construction of LALR(1), an error entry in LR(1) can replaced only by reduction, not shift.<br />\nSo, option (b), (d) are correct.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An IPv4 packet contains multiple options. The total length of options is not a multiple of 4 bytes,so padding is required. The last byte of the options area is 00.<br />\nWhich statement is TRUE regarding this byte?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It must be interpreted as a NOP used for alignment."},{"key":"B","html":"It is the EOP option, which tells the router that no more options follow."},{"key":"C","html":"It is ignored by routers because padding bytes are never processed."},{"key":"D","html":"It is used only when the IP header length exceeds 60 bytes."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar with translation rules and S is start symbol:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8a/8a5e1347aa94b3881d2b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The S.val for the root of the parse tree of expression 101.010 is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5.25"},{"key":"B","html":"5.4"},{"key":"C","html":"7.7"},{"key":"D","html":"5.2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/12f3ef20599fad137b76.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c926e27bbd6bc7ab5702.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(iii) only"},{"key":"B","html":"(i) and (iii) only"},{"key":"C","html":"(ii) only"},{"key":"D","html":"(i), (ii) and (iii)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/4548090cf059cf9cd886.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In distance-vector routing, which of the following statements about triggered updates are TRUE?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Triggered updates help reduce routing loops by quickly propagating metric changes."},{"key":"B","html":"Triggered updates can cause routing instability if sent too frequently during rapid link flapping."},{"key":"C","html":"Triggered updates eliminate the need for periodic updates."},{"key":"D","html":"Triggered updates allow routers to send updates only when a route improves, not when it becomes worse."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following graph G:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/514dc070c1b0871d993b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are correct for G?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"16 different minimum spanning tree (MST) of G possible."},{"key":"B","html":"Total weight of a MST is 10."},{"key":"C","html":"Only one edge of weight 4 is included in any MST of G."},{"key":"D","html":"On execution of Kruskal’s minimum spanning tree algorithm on G, 5<sup>th</sup> edge added in MST is either (<em>e</em>, <em>g</em>) or (<em>g</em>, <em>f</em> )."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0bf18238d9b20471593e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1e/1e1c0fb9cf0503ea7904.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Time complexity of matrix-chain multiplication algorithm using dynamic programming for n matrices is Ο(<em>n</em><sup>3</sup>)."},{"key":"B","html":"Time complexity of Bellman-Ford single-source shortest path algorithm on G(V, E) is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/137a6926ed1d5d063b6b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"Floyd-Warshall all pair shortest path algorithm on G(V, E) runs in <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/42e2cd82ea55b4c4e3a9.png\" /> time."},{"key":"D","html":"Dijkstra’s single-source shortest path algorithm on G(V, E) can run in Ο(E log V)."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>"},{"n":48,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following directed graph G(V, E):</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c2cc71327acde59c335d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Apply depth-first-search (DFS) on G with node 1 as start node, then there are more than one DFS tree possible. Minimum number of cross edges in a DFS tree (obtained above) is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/67ff01dd26b9519ce009.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following table for items with their weights and profit:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e5/e59d23278a33a195dd4f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>A person can take upto 12 kg of item. Item can be selected fractionally. The maximum profit possible for selection is ________ Rs.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[46,46],"answerText":"46","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>46</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f71eb8e67dd259fdf299.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following compound proposition is satisfiable?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f5aafc6eb65e84ed0baf.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/573bbd60ec676972c7e2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c04146f108bc588d0ebd.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e333979ae71fe881224.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":51,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a circular table with 3 seats, and there are 5 different people. Find the number of possibilities of seating 3 of these 5 people around this table? (Clockwise and anti-clockwise order are different).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[20,20],"answerText":"20","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>20</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/12912e1c216b11bb65ee.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If a graph is disconnected then its chromatic number is 1.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Chromatic number of a simple cyclic undirected graph with odd vertices is 2.<br />\nWhich statement is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> only"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> both"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/08/083454f23e169f011a9c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A strictly decreasing function from <em>R</em> to itself is one-to-one. <em>R</em> is set of real number."},{"key":"B","html":"Two functions <em>g</em> : <em>A</em> → <em>B</em> and <em>f</em> : <em>B</em> → <em>C</em>. It <em>f</em> o <em>g</em> is onto then f must also be onto."},{"key":"C","html":"A relation <em>R</em> is irreflexive, then <em>R</em><sup>2</sup> is necessarily irreflexive, where <em>R</em><sup>2</sup> = <em>R</em> o <em>R</em>."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>A</em> relation <em>R</em> is irreflexive and transitive then it is asymmetric."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c99285f1678caca60fb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/3167590f2b8a4fc04357.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e4/e495d67bb722fa676217.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b9/b96bb54444043a28e516.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8e3ef50464cc0c1e1b13.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d356b1cdea8fd52a001.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a4/a4cc6ba758a452d982cc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following matrix:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba1438cdabd5a188d65f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>One eigen value of <em>M</em> is 3. Which of the following is incorrect for <em>M</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"One eigen value of <em>M</em> is 6."},{"key":"B","html":"One eigen value of <em>M</em> is 9."},{"key":"C","html":"Eigen vector of <em>M</em> corresponding to eigen value 6 is [1 2 2]<em><sup>T</sup></em>."},{"key":"D","html":"Eigen vector of <em>M</em> corresponding to eigen value 3 is [2 –2 2]<em><sup>T</sup></em>."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eace295b3bde24d03045.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following addressing modes can be used for program relocation at run time?<br />\n(i) Indirect addressing<br />\n(ii) Relative addressing<br />\n(iii) Register addressing<br />\n(iv) Based addressing</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(ii) only"},{"key":"B","html":"(i) and (ii) only"},{"key":"C","html":"(ii) and (iv) only"},{"key":"D","html":"(i), (iii) and (iv) only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct for a pipeline?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Program counter (PC) value must pass to all stages of pipeline."},{"key":"B","html":"‘Predict never taken’ is a static branch prediction strategy."},{"key":"C","html":"‘Branch history table’ is a dynamic branch prediction technique."},{"key":"D","html":"Every pipeline stage delay must be same."}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)<br />\n(a) Not necessary<br />\n(d) Not necessary</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If topological sort not possible for a directed graph then this graph has atleast one back edge on DFS traversal.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Every cut edge of a graph connected with atleast one cut vertex.<br />\nWhich statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> only"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9eeb3eb8279c59998bab.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a sorted singly linked list L with n elements. The time complexity of applying binary search on <em>L</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(<em>n</em>)"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b00afa2273bbeede03a5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a queue Q has following element:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/68/681eae829cbeffceb7d0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Assume a program dequeue an element from Q, then it can either print element or enqueue element to Q. Program has to print all possible order of these 4 elements. How many order possible for this program? (Assume program do not go for infinite execution).</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[24,24],"answerText":"24","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>24</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3a55feef4e1b069322d5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider following two C functions:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e3aaa09ff70ce1ebdf65.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value return by fun1(28) is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[9,9],"answerText":"9","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>9</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/632d5a9dc2d8b07dec82.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a strict binary tree <em>T</em> in which every node has 2 children or no children. <em>T</em> has 31 nodes.<br />\nThe maximum possible height of T is ________ (a tree with single node has height 0).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[15,15],"answerText":"15","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>15</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/afa269724995fb403c81.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following code segment of a program in C:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/08/08cb38eaf57c394464f7.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is incorrect for above code?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"*(*<em>p</em> + 1) = 2"},{"key":"B","html":"*(**<em>q</em> + 1) = 2"},{"key":"C","html":"*<em>q</em> + 1 is equivalent to <em>A</em> + 1."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>p</em> + 1 points to address next to end of 2-dimensional array <em>A</em>."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, C, A, B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n(c) <em>p</em> + 1 = A + 1, which points to address of 2<sup>nd</sup> array of 2-dimensional array <em>A</em>.<br />\n(a) *(*<em>p</em> + 1) = 1 = *(*<em>A</em> + 1) = <em>A</em>[0][1] = 2<br />\n(b) *(**<em>q</em> + 1) = *(**&amp;<em>A</em> + 1) = *(*<em>A</em> + 1) = A[0][1] = 2</p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a binary min heap tree T in array with following elements from start of array:<br />\n7, 12, 17, 21, 15, 18, 19, 27, 22, 20, 24<br />\nInsert key 16 and then remove min element of heap. Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Root of heap after first operation (insert 16) is 7."},{"key":"B","html":"Root of heap after both operations is 12."},{"key":"C","html":"Preorder traversal of heap tree after both operations is 12, 15, 16, 21, 18, 17, 19, 27, 22, 20, 24."},{"key":"D","html":"After both operations 18 is left child of 15."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/50ef08e1e035fb760175.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/6347867d113fe87802aa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following C program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddd2e5e741aa22ad6542.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Let the address of a is 1000 in decimal and size of int is 4 bytes. The output of above program is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6 1004 1008 4"},{"key":"B","html":"8 1028 1024 1028"},{"key":"C","html":"8 1012 1020 1024"},{"key":"D","html":"6 1012 1024 8"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ce850a48efd66e6216aa.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":33,"MSQ":16,"NAT":16},"withoutKey":0}};
